## Test Smell Audit — Academic Catalog (testsmells.org)

**Target:** `DataAccess.IntegrationTests/UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`
**Framework:** MSTest 4.1 · .NET 10
**Integration calibration:** Applied — class bears `[TestCategory("Integration")]`

---

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High | 3 | 3 |
| Medium | 1 | 1 |
| Low | 0 | 0 |
| **Total** | **4** | **4** |

---

### Findings by Severity

#### 🔴 HIGH — Sleepy Test (Smell 3)

**Test:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 83)
**Rationale:** Even in integration tests, `Thread.Sleep` introduces non-determinism and inflates suite time. The calibration note downgrades Mystery Guest and Eager Test for integration scope — it does **not** exempt Sleepy Test.

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000); // ← blocks for 3 s; flaky if system is slower
```

**Fix:** Replace with a polling/retry helper or `async`-aware wait:

```csharp
await WaitUntilAsync(
    () => _repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
    timeout: TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
```

**Risk if unfixed:** 3 s unconditional delay per run; false failures under CI load; suites become slower as more such tests are added.

---

#### 🔴 HIGH — Conditional Test Logic (Smell 1)

**Test:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (line 95)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Problem:** One branch always silently passes. If `loaded` is never a `PremiumUser`, the discount-rate check is dead code — a hidden testing gap.

**Fix:** Split into two explicit tests with deterministic setup:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenStandard_ReturnsName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenPremium_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("eve@example.com", "Eve", 0.1m));
    var loaded = (PremiumUser)_repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com")!;
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** One path may never execute; regression goes undetected.

---

#### 🔴 HIGH — Assertion-Free Test / Unknown Test (Smell 4)

**Test:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (line 113)

```csharp
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    // ← no assertion
}
```

**Note:** The name implies a "does not throw" intent, but MSTest marks *any* non-throwing method as passed — this provides false confidence.

**Fix:** Assert observable outcomes:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));

    Assert.AreEqual(100, _repository.ListAll().Count);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** If `Insert` silently drops rows or corrupts data, the test still passes.

---

#### 🟡 MEDIUM — Assertion Roulette (Extended Catalog)

**Test:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (line 28)

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

Four assertions, zero message parameters. A failure shows only `Assert.AreEqual failed. Expected:<Alice>. Actual:<...>` with no indication of *which property* was being verified.

**Fix:** Add message parameters:

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should be persisted");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should be persisted");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Auto-generated ID should be positive");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Diagnosis requires reading source code instead of test output; slows triage in CI.

---

### Smell-Free Patterns ✅

| Test | Why It's Good |
|------|--------------|
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Clear arrange/act/assert; single assertion; obvious intent |
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Focused; linear flow; assertions directly related to the action |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | Count `3` is self-documenting (3 items just added); `CollectionAssert` adds value |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Properly async; clean assertion on observable outcome |

---

### Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix Effort | Impact |
|----------|-------|-----------|--------|
| 1 | Assertion-Free Test | ~2 min | Eliminates a false-pass test immediately |
| 2 | Conditional Test Logic | ~5 min | Removes dead-code branch; adds real coverage |
| 3 | Sleepy Test | ~10 min | Cuts 3 s off suite; eliminates flakiness vector |
| 4 | Assertion Roulette | ~3 min | Improves failure diagnosis in CI logs |

**Overall verdict:** 4 of 8 tests are clean and well-structured. The remaining 4 carry smells that risk false confidence (Assertion-Free), hidden gaps (Conditional Logic), flakiness (Sleepy), and slow triage (Roulette). All fixes are straightforward — the test class can be brought to a clean bill of health with modest effort.
